1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:01,651
One container is easy.

2
00:00:01,825 --> 00:00:03,102
You type docker run,

3
00:00:03,219 --> 00:00:04,356
your web app starts,

4
00:00:04,391 --> 00:00:05,529
and life is good.

5
00:00:05,854 --> 00:00:07,851
Then people actually start using it.

6
00:00:08,176 --> 00:00:09,697
Now you need three copies,

7
00:00:09,743 --> 00:00:11,589
on three servers, so you log in

8
00:00:11,636 --> 00:00:13,586
to each one and start it by hand.

9
00:00:13,911 --> 00:00:15,061
At 3 a.m.

10
00:00:15,212 --> 00:00:16,535
one of them crashes,

11
00:00:16,744 --> 00:00:18,973
and guess who gets woken up to restart it.

12
00:00:19,415 --> 00:00:20,924
A new copy needs a home,

13
00:00:21,098 --> 00:00:22,445
and you're the one who remembers

14
00:00:22,515 --> 00:00:24,674
which server still has memory free.

15
00:00:25,057 --> 00:00:26,508
Every time a copy moves,

16
00:00:26,590 --> 00:00:27,774
its address changes,

17
00:00:27,925 --> 00:00:30,038
and you go and fix the config.

18
00:00:30,421 --> 00:00:33,114
Without noticing, you've become a program.

19
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:35,158
A slow, tired one,

20
00:00:35,239 --> 00:00:36,702
whose whole job is checking

21
00:00:36,748 --> 00:00:38,165
that things are still running.

22
00:00:38,490 --> 00:00:40,615
That job is exactly what Kubernetes

23
00:00:40,649 --> 00:00:42,600
was built to take off your hands.

24
00:00:43,350 --> 00:00:44,674
So your team installs it,

25
00:00:44,755 --> 00:00:46,972
and Jordan from the platform team promises

26
00:00:47,019 --> 00:00:48,772
you'll finally sleep through the night.

27
00:00:49,097 --> 00:00:51,338
You apply your web app, three copies.

28
00:00:51,512 --> 00:00:52,986
It says: created.

29
00:00:53,265 --> 00:00:54,392
You check the pods.

30
00:00:54,635 --> 00:00:55,541
Two are running.

31
00:00:55,785 --> 00:00:57,468
The third one says Pending.

32
00:00:57,747 --> 00:00:58,687
Ten minutes later,

33
00:00:58,861 --> 00:00:59,779
still Pending.

34
00:01:00,022 --> 00:01:00,731
No error.

35
00:01:00,940 --> 00:01:01,810
No crash.

36
00:01:02,089 --> 00:01:02,635
Nothing.

37
00:01:03,076 --> 00:01:04,597
Jordan rolls their chair over,

38
00:01:04,771 --> 00:01:05,827
squints at your screen,

39
00:01:05,862 --> 00:01:07,871
and says: it's not stuck.

40
00:01:08,115 --> 00:01:09,125
It's waiting.

41
00:01:16,883 --> 00:01:17,963
Waiting for what?

42
00:01:18,288 --> 00:01:19,693
You go looking for logs,

43
00:01:19,716 --> 00:01:20,691
and there are none,

44
00:01:20,807 --> 00:01:23,094
because that copy never started anywhere.

45
00:01:23,477 --> 00:01:25,323
For my first year with Kubernetes,

46
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:27,332
I treated it like a very expensive

47
00:01:27,367 --> 00:01:29,015
remote control for docker run.

48
00:01:29,341 --> 00:01:31,221
The same thing I used to do by hand,

49
00:01:31,395 --> 00:01:33,149
just on every server at once.

50
00:01:33,474 --> 00:01:35,413
Press apply, things start.

51
00:01:35,691 --> 00:01:36,713
If that were true,

52
00:01:36,887 --> 00:01:38,338
created would mean running,

53
00:01:38,489 --> 00:01:39,662
and anything that didn't

54
00:01:39,720 --> 00:01:41,043
run would come with an error.

55
00:01:41,322 --> 00:01:42,657
That picture is wrong,

56
00:01:42,785 --> 00:01:44,817
and it's why Kubernetes feels like magic

57
00:01:44,886 --> 00:01:47,266
on good days and haunted on bad ones.

58
00:01:47,591 --> 00:01:48,845
By the end of this video,

59
00:01:48,961 --> 00:01:50,204
you'll know exactly what

60
00:01:50,238 --> 00:01:51,585
that pod is waiting for,

61
00:01:51,794 --> 00:01:53,164
and why it's the same thing

62
00:01:53,211 --> 00:01:55,057
that finally lets you sleep.

63
00:01:55,817 --> 00:01:57,059
So let's pretend we're building

64
00:01:57,106 --> 00:01:58,371
Kubernetes ourselves,

65
00:01:58,452 --> 00:01:59,869
to take over your 3 a.m.

66
00:01:59,892 --> 00:02:00,368
job.

67
00:02:00,751 --> 00:02:03,027
Same web app, same three servers.

68
00:02:03,468 --> 00:02:05,035
Except we stop thinking of them as

69
00:02:05,128 --> 00:02:07,252
three separate machines you log in to.

70
00:02:07,577 --> 00:02:08,599
They're one group,

71
00:02:08,773 --> 00:02:10,016
managed together, and

72
00:02:10,074 --> 00:02:11,316
that group has a name:

73
00:02:11,560 --> 00:02:12,349
a cluster.

74
00:02:12,674 --> 00:02:14,520
Ours is three servers.

75
00:02:14,845 --> 00:02:15,971
Every chore you did by

76
00:02:16,053 --> 00:02:17,701
hand becomes a requirement.

77
00:02:18,142 --> 00:02:20,848
One: keep three copies of the app running,

78
00:02:20,871 --> 00:02:21,637
always.

79
00:02:21,962 --> 00:02:23,437
Two: pick a server

80
00:02:23,495 --> 00:02:24,969
with room for each copy,

81
00:02:24,993 --> 00:02:26,943
without anyone choosing by hand.

82
00:02:27,454 --> 00:02:29,532
Three: heal on its own,

83
00:02:29,683 --> 00:02:32,005
even when a server dies at 3 a.m.

84
00:02:32,388 --> 00:02:34,524
And four: one address that reaches

85
00:02:34,559 --> 00:02:36,057
whichever copies are alive,

86
00:02:36,173 --> 00:02:38,820
so nobody edits config when they move.

87
00:02:39,583 --> 00:02:41,092
The obvious first try is

88
00:02:41,139 --> 00:02:42,706
to automate your own hands:

89
00:02:43,031 --> 00:02:43,821
a script.

90
00:02:44,204 --> 00:02:45,899
For each server, connect,

91
00:02:45,945 --> 00:02:47,071
and start the app.

92
00:02:47,397 --> 00:02:48,407
Three copies.

93
00:02:48,732 --> 00:02:50,078
It even picks the servers,

94
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:52,679
sort of: we typed their names in by hand,

95
00:02:52,714 --> 00:02:54,722
and it never checked if they had room.

96
00:02:54,966 --> 00:02:55,953
That doesn't count.

97
00:02:56,278 --> 00:02:58,879
Now server two dies at 3 a.m.

98
00:02:59,157 --> 00:03:01,038
The script finished hours ago.

99
00:03:01,282 --> 00:03:03,209
It printed done, and went home.

100
00:03:03,488 --> 00:03:04,614
You have two copies,

101
00:03:04,649 --> 00:03:05,845
and nobody knows.

102
00:03:06,019 --> 00:03:07,702
And there's no single address,

103
00:03:07,795 --> 00:03:09,026
just three server names

104
00:03:09,060 --> 00:03:10,790
you hand out and hope.

105
00:03:11,550 --> 00:03:12,665
You might be thinking:

106
00:03:12,990 --> 00:03:14,661
just add a restart policy.

107
00:03:14,987 --> 00:03:15,509
Sure.

108
00:03:15,892 --> 00:03:18,052
Docker will restart a crashed container,

109
00:03:18,110 --> 00:03:19,236
on the same machine.

110
00:03:19,619 --> 00:03:21,511
But at 3 a.m., the machine

111
00:03:21,558 --> 00:03:22,847
is the thing that died,

112
00:03:23,021 --> 00:03:25,284
and its restart policy died with it.

113
00:03:25,667 --> 00:03:27,281
Both fixes act once,

114
00:03:27,362 --> 00:03:29,290
and then nothing is watching.

115
00:03:30,050 --> 00:03:30,712
Okay.

116
00:03:31,095 --> 00:03:32,720
Let's look under the abstraction,

117
00:03:32,778 --> 00:03:34,334
and ask a better question.

118
00:03:34,613 --> 00:03:35,843
The script answers:

119
00:03:36,052 --> 00:03:37,782
how do I start three copies?

120
00:03:38,165 --> 00:03:39,442
The better question is:

121
00:03:39,617 --> 00:03:41,869
how do I make sure there are always three?

122
00:03:42,252 --> 00:03:43,820
Starting happens once.

123
00:03:44,098 --> 00:03:46,409
Always means someone keeps checking.

124
00:03:46,920 --> 00:03:48,266
At the start of this video,

125
00:03:48,324 --> 00:03:49,648
that someone was you.

126
00:03:50,031 --> 00:03:51,378
We need a thermostat.

127
00:03:51,761 --> 00:03:54,454
You set what you want: 21 degrees.

128
00:03:54,780 --> 00:03:57,334
It measures what you have: 19.

129
00:03:57,612 --> 00:03:59,238
And it acts on the difference.

130
00:03:59,563 --> 00:04:00,468
Not once.

131
00:04:00,852 --> 00:04:02,094
Forever.

132
00:04:02,850 --> 00:04:05,276
Kubernetes is built out of thermostats.

133
00:04:05,602 --> 00:04:07,215
Each one is a small program

134
00:04:07,285 --> 00:04:08,434
called a controller,

135
00:04:08,609 --> 00:04:10,257
running three steps in a loop.

136
00:04:10,640 --> 00:04:11,592
Read what you want.

137
00:04:11,871 --> 00:04:13,415
Compare it with what you have.

138
00:04:13,740 --> 00:04:14,855
Fix the difference.

139
00:04:15,180 --> 00:04:15,923
Then again.

140
00:04:16,248 --> 00:04:17,188
And again.

141
00:04:17,572 --> 00:04:19,464
Want three, have two?

142
00:04:19,789 --> 00:04:21,391
Act: plus one.

143
00:04:21,716 --> 00:04:23,795
I call it want versus have,

144
00:04:23,969 --> 00:04:25,339
and honestly, it's the

145
00:04:25,455 --> 00:04:27,196
only idea in this video.

146
00:04:27,521 --> 00:04:29,170
A controller never finishes,

147
00:04:29,240 --> 00:04:30,273
and it never trusts

148
00:04:30,319 --> 00:04:32,014
that things stayed fixed.

149
00:04:32,767 --> 00:04:34,416
So where does the want live?

150
00:04:34,741 --> 00:04:37,341
In a file: a deployment called web,

151
00:04:37,423 --> 00:04:38,735
with three replicas.

152
00:04:39,060 --> 00:04:40,917
You run kube control apply.

153
00:04:41,300 --> 00:04:42,415
And now my favourite

154
00:04:42,450 --> 00:04:43,808
fact about Kubernetes.

155
00:04:44,249 --> 00:04:46,177
Apply doesn't start anything.

156
00:04:46,455 --> 00:04:48,452
It sends your file to one program,

157
00:04:48,510 --> 00:04:50,414
the API server, which saves

158
00:04:50,461 --> 00:04:52,237
it in a database and replies:

159
00:04:52,562 --> 00:04:53,189
OK.

160
00:04:53,514 --> 00:04:55,047
Zero containers are running.

161
00:04:55,372 --> 00:04:57,322
All you've done is write down a wish.

162
00:04:57,647 --> 00:04:58,599
So that created from

163
00:04:58,646 --> 00:04:59,772
the start of the video?

164
00:05:00,155 --> 00:05:02,280
It means the wish was written down.

165
00:05:02,663 --> 00:05:03,487
Nothing more.

166
00:05:03,928 --> 00:05:06,355
But two of my pods did start, you say.

167
00:05:06,680 --> 00:05:07,423
They did.

168
00:05:07,806 --> 00:05:10,012
Just not because of apply.

169
00:05:10,767 --> 00:05:13,031
The wish wakes up the first thermostat:

170
00:05:13,182 --> 00:05:14,482
the replica controller.

171
00:05:14,865 --> 00:05:16,096
It reads the notebook.

172
00:05:16,421 --> 00:05:18,615
Want: three copies of web.

173
00:05:18,998 --> 00:05:20,543
Have: zero.

174
00:05:20,868 --> 00:05:22,621
So it creates three pods.

175
00:05:23,004 --> 00:05:24,513
Pod is Kubernetes' word

176
00:05:24,571 --> 00:05:26,185
for one copy of your app.

177
00:05:26,568 --> 00:05:28,495
It never handles a bare container.

178
00:05:28,878 --> 00:05:30,248
It wraps it in a pod,

179
00:05:30,422 --> 00:05:32,059
usually just one container,

180
00:05:32,268 --> 00:05:33,743
sometimes with a small helper

181
00:05:33,789 --> 00:05:35,426
that has to live and die with it,

182
00:05:35,635 --> 00:05:37,458
and the pod gets its own address.

183
00:05:37,783 --> 00:05:41,045
So for us, one pod is one copy of web,

184
00:05:41,196 --> 00:05:42,322
and we want three.

185
00:05:42,705 --> 00:05:43,727
But look closer.

186
00:05:44,052 --> 00:05:45,515
It didn't run anything either.

187
00:05:45,898 --> 00:05:46,943
It wrote three more

188
00:05:47,001 --> 00:05:48,267
entries in the notebook.

189
00:05:48,650 --> 00:05:51,366
Kubernetes is extremely good at paperwork.

190
00:05:51,610 --> 00:05:53,154
And remember our Pending pod?

191
00:05:53,363 --> 00:05:54,385
It exists.

192
00:05:54,710 --> 00:05:55,790
It's a perfectly good

193
00:05:55,848 --> 00:05:57,091
entry in this notebook.

194
00:05:57,334 --> 00:05:59,436
It's just missing one thing.

195
00:06:00,200 --> 00:06:02,255
Each new pod has an empty field,

196
00:06:02,429 --> 00:06:03,544
called node.

197
00:06:03,927 --> 00:06:06,109
A node is Kubernetes' word for one

198
00:06:06,156 --> 00:06:08,548
machine in the cluster that can run pods.

199
00:06:08,873 --> 00:06:10,614
A physical server in a rack,

200
00:06:10,730 --> 00:06:12,298
or far more often a virtual

201
00:06:12,379 --> 00:06:13,737
one you rent from a cloud.

202
00:06:14,120 --> 00:06:16,199
Our three servers are three nodes.

203
00:06:16,640 --> 00:06:17,731
The next thermostat,

204
00:06:17,778 --> 00:06:19,264
the scheduler, watches for

205
00:06:19,345 --> 00:06:20,947
pods with that field blank.

206
00:06:21,272 --> 00:06:23,165
It checks how much room each server has

207
00:06:23,246 --> 00:06:25,463
left against how much the pod asks for,

208
00:06:25,614 --> 00:06:26,694
picks the best fit,

209
00:06:26,903 --> 00:06:27,995
and writes that server's

210
00:06:28,041 --> 00:06:29,191
name into the blank.

211
00:06:29,434 --> 00:06:31,443
That's the scheduler's entire job.

212
00:06:31,722 --> 00:06:32,975
It doesn't start anything.

213
00:06:33,300 --> 00:06:34,798
It fills in one blank.

214
00:06:35,123 --> 00:06:36,888
As long as some server has room.

215
00:06:37,271 --> 00:06:38,107
Hold that thought.

216
00:06:38,432 --> 00:06:40,661
And nobody typed a server name by hand,

217
00:06:40,835 --> 00:06:42,182
least of all you.

218
00:06:42,933 --> 00:06:44,942
So who actually runs anything?

219
00:06:45,267 --> 00:06:46,613
Every server has a small

220
00:06:46,683 --> 00:06:48,274
agent called the kube-let.

221
00:06:48,599 --> 00:06:49,702
Another thermostat,

222
00:06:49,818 --> 00:06:51,838
but it only cares about its own server.

223
00:06:52,163 --> 00:06:54,578
Want: every pod with my name on it.

224
00:06:54,822 --> 00:06:57,376
Have: what's running on me right now.

225
00:06:57,759 --> 00:06:59,535
Want one, have zero,

226
00:06:59,779 --> 00:07:00,928
so it tells the container

227
00:07:00,998 --> 00:07:02,368
engine to start one.

228
00:07:02,751 --> 00:07:04,179
That container is the same

229
00:07:04,225 --> 00:07:05,781
thing from our Docker video:

230
00:07:06,106 --> 00:07:07,523
an ordinary process,

231
00:07:07,569 --> 00:07:08,637
with blinders on.

232
00:07:08,962 --> 00:07:11,075
Three servers, three copies.

233
00:07:11,586 --> 00:07:13,385
After a file, a notebook

234
00:07:13,455 --> 00:07:14,930
and three thermostats,

235
00:07:15,255 --> 00:07:17,577
something has finally started.

236
00:07:18,333 --> 00:07:20,086
Now notice who talked to whom.

237
00:07:20,469 --> 00:07:22,733
The scheduler never called a kube-let.

238
00:07:23,116 --> 00:07:24,277
The replica controller

239
00:07:24,393 --> 00:07:25,729
never called the scheduler.

240
00:07:26,007 --> 00:07:27,366
Nobody calls anybody.

241
00:07:27,644 --> 00:07:29,618
They only read and write the notebook,

242
00:07:29,734 --> 00:07:31,406
and react to what changes.

243
00:07:31,731 --> 00:07:32,938
That's deliberate.

244
00:07:33,380 --> 00:07:36,688
In 2016, the Google engineers behind Borg,

245
00:07:36,805 --> 00:07:38,221
Kubernetes' ancestor,

246
00:07:38,337 --> 00:07:39,591
wrote a paper about it.

247
00:07:39,974 --> 00:07:41,983
They called the design choreography,

248
00:07:42,064 --> 00:07:43,178
not orchestration.

249
00:07:43,562 --> 00:07:45,338
No conductor shouting cues.

250
00:07:45,663 --> 00:07:48,461
Just dancers, all watching the same floor.

251
00:07:48,705 --> 00:07:49,947
Kill any one of them,

252
00:07:50,156 --> 00:07:51,608
restart it, and it reads

253
00:07:51,654 --> 00:07:53,721
the notebook and carries on.

254
00:07:54,483 --> 00:07:55,400
So where do all these

255
00:07:55,435 --> 00:07:57,072
thermostats actually live?

256
00:07:57,397 --> 00:07:58,570
Look at the hub again,

257
00:07:58,814 --> 00:08:00,787
because it splits cleanly in two.

258
00:08:01,170 --> 00:08:03,121
The notebook, the replica controller

259
00:08:03,179 --> 00:08:05,280
and the scheduler never run your app.

260
00:08:05,524 --> 00:08:06,917
They only do paperwork.

261
00:08:07,196 --> 00:08:09,007
Kubernetes keeps them together,

262
00:08:09,158 --> 00:08:10,842
usually on machines of their own,

263
00:08:10,958 --> 00:08:13,210
and calls that group the control plane.

264
00:08:13,651 --> 00:08:15,277
I think of it as the office.

265
00:08:15,555 --> 00:08:17,901
It decides what should exist and where.

266
00:08:18,342 --> 00:08:20,188
The nodes are the workshop floor:

267
00:08:20,397 --> 00:08:21,999
each one has its kube-let,

268
00:08:22,173 --> 00:08:22,893
and that's where your

269
00:08:22,928 --> 00:08:24,356
containers actually run.

270
00:08:24,867 --> 00:08:26,376
Office plus floor.

271
00:08:26,701 --> 00:08:28,141
That's the whole cluster.

272
00:08:28,524 --> 00:08:30,254
And on a managed cloud cluster,

273
00:08:30,370 --> 00:08:32,494
you often never see the office at all.

274
00:08:32,878 --> 00:08:34,050
The provider runs it,

275
00:08:34,178 --> 00:08:35,722
and you only get the floor.

276
00:08:36,001 --> 00:08:38,451
Which is why, when a node goes quiet,

277
00:08:38,625 --> 00:08:40,378
the floor can't fix itself.

278
00:08:40,761 --> 00:08:42,781
The office has to notice.

279
00:08:43,533 --> 00:08:45,588
Which brings us back to 3 a.m.

280
00:08:46,029 --> 00:08:47,411
Server two dies.

281
00:08:47,794 --> 00:08:49,837
Its kube-let stops checking in.

282
00:08:50,220 --> 00:08:52,043
Another thermostat in the office,

283
00:08:52,159 --> 00:08:53,204
the node controller,

284
00:08:53,378 --> 00:08:54,644
notices the silence and

285
00:08:54,713 --> 00:08:56,618
marks server two unreachable.

286
00:08:56,943 --> 00:08:58,510
Then it waits, about

287
00:08:58,592 --> 00:09:00,020
five minutes by default,

288
00:09:00,171 --> 00:09:01,924
in case it's only a bad moment.

289
00:09:02,249 --> 00:09:03,688
After that, its pods

290
00:09:03,712 --> 00:09:05,058
are struck from the books.

291
00:09:05,302 --> 00:09:07,415
And the replica controller does its thing.

292
00:09:07,659 --> 00:09:09,389
Want three, have two.

293
00:09:09,772 --> 00:09:10,654
Plus one.

294
00:09:11,037 --> 00:09:12,871
The scheduler fills in a server,

295
00:09:13,022 --> 00:09:14,532
a kube-let starts it.

296
00:09:14,915 --> 00:09:15,902
Nobody woke up.

297
00:09:16,180 --> 00:09:18,142
Not you, not even Jordan.

298
00:09:18,525 --> 00:09:19,849
A dead server is just

299
00:09:19,895 --> 00:09:22,078
a very large bug report.

300
00:09:22,833 --> 00:09:25,074
One box left: one address.

301
00:09:25,399 --> 00:09:27,175
Pods come and go, and each

302
00:09:27,268 --> 00:09:28,975
new one gets a new address.

303
00:09:29,300 --> 00:09:31,657
That's the config you used to fix by hand.

304
00:09:31,982 --> 00:09:33,294
So you add a Service:

305
00:09:33,537 --> 00:09:35,441
one stable name, web,

306
00:09:35,685 --> 00:09:36,765
meaning whichever web

307
00:09:36,823 --> 00:09:38,298
pods are alive right now.

308
00:09:38,541 --> 00:09:39,737
You'd expect a program

309
00:09:39,830 --> 00:09:41,038
sitting at that address,

310
00:09:41,130 --> 00:09:42,477
passing traffic along.

311
00:09:42,802 --> 00:09:43,267
Nope.

312
00:09:43,778 --> 00:09:46,250
On every server, one more thermostat,

313
00:09:46,367 --> 00:09:48,898
kube proxy, reads the notebook and writes

314
00:09:48,967 --> 00:09:51,011
packet rules into the Linux kernel:

315
00:09:51,220 --> 00:09:53,692
traffic for web goes to one of these pods.

316
00:09:53,901 --> 00:09:55,689
The kernel forwards it itself.

317
00:09:55,933 --> 00:09:57,245
So despite the name,

318
00:09:57,489 --> 00:10:00,194
kube proxy usually proxies nothing.

319
00:10:00,519 --> 00:10:01,854
When a pod is replaced,

320
00:10:01,947 --> 00:10:03,979
it just rewrites the rules.

321
00:10:04,733 --> 00:10:06,405
This design isn't free.

322
00:10:06,730 --> 00:10:07,624
Here's the bill.

323
00:10:08,007 --> 00:10:09,365
Nobody is in charge,

324
00:10:09,540 --> 00:10:10,956
so when something breaks,

325
00:10:11,130 --> 00:10:12,953
no single place says why.

326
00:10:13,336 --> 00:10:14,416
The answer is scattered

327
00:10:14,439 --> 00:10:15,832
across a dozen loops,

328
00:10:15,983 --> 00:10:18,107
each doing exactly what it was told.

329
00:10:18,491 --> 00:10:20,174
Healing isn't instant either:

330
00:10:20,348 --> 00:10:22,043
we just waited five minutes.

331
00:10:22,252 --> 00:10:23,692
And it needs spare room.

332
00:10:24,075 --> 00:10:25,433
If no server has space

333
00:10:25,468 --> 00:10:26,397
for the replacement,

334
00:10:26,490 --> 00:10:28,266
it just waits, Pending,

335
00:10:28,324 --> 00:10:29,636
like the pod from our hook.

336
00:10:29,961 --> 00:10:31,749
And for one app on one server,

337
00:10:31,865 --> 00:10:33,235
it's a lot of machinery.

338
00:10:33,560 --> 00:10:34,860
A script and a restart

339
00:10:34,907 --> 00:10:36,439
policy would honestly do.

340
00:10:36,764 --> 00:10:39,203
But across many servers at 3 a.m.,

341
00:10:39,319 --> 00:10:41,884
I'll take a room full of stubborn thermostats

342
00:10:41,943 --> 00:10:43,881
over a script that went home.

343
00:10:44,633 --> 00:10:45,968
Let's put it all together.

344
00:10:46,212 --> 00:10:47,361
Apply writes what you

345
00:10:47,431 --> 00:10:48,673
want into the notebook.

346
00:10:48,882 --> 00:10:51,135
Controllers compare it with what you have.

347
00:10:51,378 --> 00:10:53,143
The scheduler fills in a server.

348
00:10:53,352 --> 00:10:54,931
The kube-let makes it real.

349
00:10:55,256 --> 00:10:57,938
And kube proxy points the address at it.

350
00:10:58,263 --> 00:10:59,981
Kubernetes isn't a launcher.

351
00:11:00,155 --> 00:11:01,363
It's a set of loops,

352
00:11:01,409 --> 00:11:02,524
comparing what you asked

353
00:11:02,570 --> 00:11:03,963
for with what exists,

354
00:11:04,114 --> 00:11:06,041
and fixing the difference.

355
00:11:06,800 --> 00:11:09,505
Now, back to the pod that said Pending.

356
00:11:09,830 --> 00:11:11,897
The replica controller did its part:

357
00:11:12,222 --> 00:11:13,267
three entries.

358
00:11:13,545 --> 00:11:15,008
Then the scheduler looked.

359
00:11:15,333 --> 00:11:16,483
Each copy asks for

360
00:11:16,552 --> 00:11:18,097
two gigabytes of memory.

361
00:11:18,340 --> 00:11:20,523
Two copies found a server with room.

362
00:11:20,767 --> 00:11:23,112
For the third, every server was already

363
00:11:23,182 --> 00:11:25,516
filled past that by other teams' apps.

364
00:11:25,759 --> 00:11:27,478
So its blank stayed blank.

365
00:11:27,803 --> 00:11:29,823
No error, because nothing failed.

366
00:11:30,148 --> 00:11:31,971
The scheduler wrote a note on the pod,

367
00:11:32,005 --> 00:11:33,004
and kept trying.

368
00:11:33,248 --> 00:11:35,709
So change the want, or the have.

369
00:11:36,034 --> 00:11:37,230
Add a fourth server,

370
00:11:37,276 --> 00:11:38,414
and once it joins,

371
00:11:38,530 --> 00:11:40,121
the scheduler fills in the blank,

372
00:11:40,144 --> 00:11:41,456
and the pod starts.

373
00:11:41,781 --> 00:11:43,314
You didn't apply anything.

374
00:11:43,639 --> 00:11:44,974
On a cloud, there's even

375
00:11:45,020 --> 00:11:46,228
a thermostat for that:

376
00:11:46,553 --> 00:11:48,399
a cluster autoscaler watches for

377
00:11:48,468 --> 00:11:50,651
Pending pods and rents another server.

378
00:11:51,034 --> 00:11:53,147
Finding room used to be your job too.

379
00:11:53,530 --> 00:11:54,947
Jordan was right, by the way.

380
00:11:55,272 --> 00:11:56,328
It wasn't stuck.

381
00:11:56,653 --> 00:11:57,977
It was waiting for a want

382
00:11:58,023 --> 00:11:59,649
it could actually have.

383
00:12:00,400 --> 00:12:01,944
Want to see that note yourself?

384
00:12:02,385 --> 00:12:03,941
Run kube control describe

385
00:12:03,987 --> 00:12:05,741
pod on any Pending pod,

386
00:12:05,799 --> 00:12:07,145
and scroll to the bottom.

387
00:12:07,389 --> 00:12:09,514
The scheduler tells you exactly why:

388
00:12:09,792 --> 00:12:11,325
something like, zero of

389
00:12:11,406 --> 00:12:12,823
three nodes are available,

390
00:12:13,067 --> 00:12:14,286
insufficient memory.

391
00:12:14,529 --> 00:12:16,016
And to watch the loop at work,

392
00:12:16,190 --> 00:12:18,117
run kube control get pods,

393
00:12:18,233 --> 00:12:19,766
dash w, for watch,

394
00:12:19,882 --> 00:12:21,925
and delete a pod from a second terminal.

395
00:12:22,169 --> 00:12:23,446
A new name appears before

396
00:12:23,481 --> 00:12:24,793
the old one is even gone.

397
00:12:25,002 --> 00:12:27,091
You just filed a bug report to a loop.

398
00:12:27,300 --> 00:12:29,390
Kubernetes never does what you say.

399
00:12:29,541 --> 00:12:30,690
It does what you want,

400
00:12:30,807 --> 00:12:32,060
as soon as it can.

401
00:12:32,235 --> 00:12:34,336
Have you ever stared at a Pending pod,

402
00:12:34,487 --> 00:12:36,403
waiting for an error that never came?

403
00:12:36,646 --> 00:12:38,295
What turned out to be missing?

404
00:12:39,050 --> 00:12:40,710
To summarise everything.

405
00:12:41,035 --> 00:12:42,881
Apply doesn't start anything.

406
00:12:43,206 --> 00:12:45,737
It writes the want into the API server,

407
00:12:45,830 --> 00:12:46,956
the shared notebook.

408
00:12:47,340 --> 00:12:49,441
Controllers compare want with have,

409
00:12:49,511 --> 00:12:51,647
and fix the difference, forever.

410
00:12:52,088 --> 00:12:54,178
The scheduler fills in a server name,

411
00:12:54,271 --> 00:12:56,732
and that server's kube-let makes it real.

412
00:12:57,115 --> 00:12:59,228
A Pending pod isn't an error.

413
00:12:59,553 --> 00:13:01,713
It's a want that nothing can have yet,

414
00:13:01,922 --> 00:13:04,151
so change the want, or the have.

415
00:13:04,534 --> 00:13:06,322
Kubernetes isn't a launcher.

416
00:13:06,647 --> 00:13:09,340
It's a room full of stubborn thermostats,

417
00:13:09,491 --> 00:13:12,162
doing the job you used to do at 3 a.m.

418
00:13:12,917 --> 00:13:14,113
If you liked the video,

419
00:13:14,229 --> 00:13:15,703
drop a like and subscribe,

420
00:13:15,819 --> 00:13:16,957
and tell me what topics

421
00:13:16,992 --> 00:13:18,304
you want to see next.
